 "result": "This is a Flask-style fork with renamed identifiers (package `tessel`).\n\n**How the variables get there**\n- `_base_template_vars()` in `src/tessel/templating.py:21` is the default context injector. It returns `g` and, if there is a request, `request`. `session` is deliberately not replaced: the proxy stays so access is tracked.\n- `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:184` pre-registers it: `template_ctx_injectors = defaultdict(list, {None: [_base_template_vars]})`.\n- `fill_template_scope()` in `src/tessel/app.py:588` runs the injectors for the app (`None`) plus the current request's bundles, calling each with `context.update(...)`. It then re-applies the caller's original values so `render_template` arguments win.\n- The docstring there also mentions `config`. Jinja's own globals supply that, and `session` comes from the global proxies.\n\n**Adding your own**\n- Decorate a function that returns a dict with `@app.ctx_injector` (`Scaffold.ctx_injector`, `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:549`, wrapped in `@setupmethod`). This is the equivalent of Flask's `context_processor`.\n- On a bundle, `@bundle.ctx_injector` only affects that bundle's views. Use `Bundle.app_ctx_injector` (`src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py`) to inject into every template from a bundle.",
